    think:{quote:"Standing quietly on the Radley porch, Scout finally looks out at her own street from Boo's point of view, remembering her father's old advice about imagining another person's life.", question:"Why is standing on the Radley porch such an important final moment for Scout?", options:["Because she finally sees her own street the way Boo has seen it for years, understanding him completely.","Because she wants to move into the Radley house herself.","Because it is the only place with a clear view of the school."], answer:0, note:"This moment completes the lesson Atticus gave Scout at the very beginning: understanding people means imagining life from where they stand."},
