const READINGS = {
1:[
"Scout Finch was six years old and lived in the small town of Maycomb, Alabama, with her older brother Jem and their father, Atticus, who worked as a lawyer. Their mother had died when Scout was very young, so a kind cook named Calpurnia looked after the children and ran the house.",
"Every summer a small boy named Dill came to stay next door, and he quickly became a friend of Scout and Jem. The three children spent hot summer afternoons playing on the porch and in the yard, telling each other stories.",
"Not far from the Finch house stood the Radley house, a dark and mysterious place that frightened the neighborhood children. People said a grown man named Boo Radley lived inside but never came out, and nobody in Maycomb had seen him in years.",
"In September, Scout started school for the first time. Her teacher, Miss Caroline, became angry when she learned that Scout could already read, because she thought this was against the rules of teaching.",
"At school, Scout noticed a poor boy named Walter Cunningham, whose family had no money for lunch, and a dirty, unhappy boy named Burris Ewell, who had lice in his hair and did not come to school very often.",
"That evening, Scout complained to Atticus about her bad day. Atticus gave her some quiet advice: he said a person never really understands another person until they imagine standing in that person's shoes and walking around in them.",
"Scout did not fully understand her father's advice yet, but she remembered it, and it slowly began to change the way she looked at her neighbors in Maycomb."
],
2:[
"One day, Scout and Jem found a strange gift inside a knothole of the old oak tree beside the Radley house: two sticks of chewing gum, wrapped carefully in silver paper. Soon more gifts appeared in the same knothole, including one old penny and then a second penny, both dark with age.",
"Excited and curious, Jem invented a game about the Radley family, and the three children acted out scenes from Boo Radley's sad story again and again during the summer. One afternoon, Atticus caught them playing the game and told them, firmly, to stop it at once.",
"Scout often visited her neighbor Miss Maudie, a friendly woman who loved working in her garden. Miss Maudie explained that Boo Radley had not always been so hidden away; as a young man he had been polite and normal, until his strict family shut him inside the house many years before.",
"On Dill's last night in Maycomb before he went home, the three children decided to be brave and sneak across the yard to look through a window on the Radley porch.",
"Suddenly, a loud shotgun fired inside the house, and the frightened children ran for their lives. Climbing back through a fence, Jem's trousers caught on the wire, and he had to leave them behind to escape safely.",
"Later that night, unable to sleep, Jem quietly left the house and went back alone in the dark to fetch his trousers from the fence, because he could not explain their loss to Atticus without also explaining the secret game.",
"When Jem returned, he told Scout a strange thing: he had found his trousers folded neatly over the fence, as if someone inside the Radley house had been expecting him."
],
3:[
"When Jem went back to the fence that night, he found that someone had taken the time to mend his torn trousers, which now lay neatly folded, waiting for him.",
"More small gifts kept appearing in the knothole of the Radley oak: two soap dolls that looked just like Jem and Scout, an old watch that no longer worked, and a shining medal. Then, one day, Jem and Scout found the knothole filled with cement, because Nathan Radley said the tree was dying.",
"That winter, the town of Maycomb had rare, light snow, and the children built a small, strange-looking snowman using mud and only a thin layer of snow.",
"One cold night, Miss Maudie's house caught fire and burned for hours while the whole street watched, and the children stood outside wrapped in blankets against the freezing air.",
"Later, Scout realized that someone had quietly placed a warm blanket around her shoulders while she watched the fire, and Atticus guessed that the blanket had come from Boo Radley himself.",
"Around this time, Atticus agreed to defend Tom Robinson, a black man from Maycomb, in an important court case, even though he knew many people in town would insult him for it.",
"At Christmas, the family visited relatives at Finch's Landing, where their cousin Francis repeated ugly insults about Atticus that he had heard from other adults. Furious, Scout got into a fight with Francis to defend her father's name.",
"Afterward, their kind Uncle Jack listened carefully to Scout's side of the story, and he began to understand why she had felt she had no choice but to fight."
],
4:[
"Jem and Scout thought their father Atticus was old and dull, because unlike other fathers he did not hunt or play football, and he never talked about his own skills.",
"One day, a sick dog ran wildly down their street, and the neighbors realized with fear that the dog was mad and dangerous. The sheriff handed Atticus a rifle, and to everyone's surprise, Atticus took careful aim and shot the dog dead with a single shot.",
"From that day on, people in Maycomb called Atticus 'One-Shot Finch,' and Jem and Scout felt proud of their father in a completely new way.",
"Miss Maudie later explained an important rule to the children: harming a mockingbird is wrong, because mockingbirds never hurt anyone and only sing beautiful songs for people to enjoy.",
"An elderly, unpleasant neighbor named Mrs. Dubose often sat on her porch and shouted cruel insults at Jem and Scout, especially about their father's decision to defend a black man in court.",
"Furious at her words, Jem angrily destroyed all of Mrs. Dubose's camellia flowers with a stick, so Atticus decided to punish him in an unusual way: Jem had to visit her house and read aloud to her every afternoon for weeks.",
"Not long after the reading sessions ended, Mrs. Dubose died. Atticus then explained that she had secretly been fighting a serious addiction to a painkilling medicine, and had bravely chosen to stop using it completely before she died, suffering great pain instead.",
"Atticus told Jem that real courage is not a man with a rifle in his hand, but someone who keeps fighting for what is right even when they know they cannot win."
],
5:[
"While Atticus was away for work one Sunday, Calpurnia took Jem and Scout to visit her own church, in the black part of Maycomb, for the very first time.",
"At the church, a woman named Lula was very rude, loudly complaining that white children should not be there, but the kind Reverend Sykes made Jem and Scout feel completely welcome as honored guests.",
"During the service, Reverend Sykes asked everyone to collect money for the family of Tom Robinson, since Tom could not work while he waited for his trial.",
"Listening to the powerful sermon and watching the church members care so generously for each other, Scout began to understand that Calpurnia had a whole other life and family outside their own house.",
"Soon afterward, their strict Aunt Alexandra came to live with the family, saying that a house full of men needed a woman's feminine influence.",
"Aunt Alexandra often lectured Jem and Scout about the importance of family pride, insisting that the Finches were better than most families in Maycomb because of their old and respected name.",
"Around this time, their friend Dill ran away from his home and secretly traveled all the way to Maycomb by himself.",
"That night, Scout was shocked to find that Dill had chosen to hide quietly under her bed, tired, hungry, and afraid to go back home."
],
6:[
"One night, a group of men from the county drove to the jail where Tom Robinson was being held, planning to take him away before his trial even began.",
"Atticus sat calmly outside the jail door, to guard it alone, when Jem, Scout, and Dill suddenly appeared, worried about their father's safety.",
"Not recognizing the danger, Scout began talking politely to Mr. Cunningham, one of the men in the crowd, about his son and ordinary Maycomb life.",
"Scout's innocent, friendly words made Mr. Cunningham suddenly feel ashamed of what the men had planned to do, and he quietly told the others to leave.",
"Soon after, the trial began, and it seemed like the whole county had come to the courthouse to watch. Jem, Scout, and Dill found seats in the balcony with their friend Reverend Sykes, looking down at the crowded courtroom below.",
"The sheriff, Mr. Heck Tate, was the first witness to testify, describing exactly what he found on the night Mayella Ewell said she was attacked.",
"Then Bob Ewell testified, angrily insisting that Tom Robinson had hurt his daughter, though his evidence did not always seem to match Heck Tate's careful description of events.",
"Watching closely, Atticus showed the jury an important piece of evidence: Mayella had been beaten mainly on the right side of her face, which suggested that whoever attacked her was left-handed, just like Bob Ewell himself."
],
7:[
"Mayella Ewell testified next, seeming nervous and lonely as she answered Atticus's careful questions about the night of the attack.",
"Then Tom Robinson took the stand himself, calmly explaining that Mayella had invited him inside, kissed him suddenly, and that her father had seen them through the window.",
"Tom caused real shock in the courtroom when he said that he had felt sorry for Mayella because her life seemed so hard and lonely, since in Maycomb a black man was never supposed to feel sorry for a white person.",
"Watching the lawyer treat Tom with obvious contempt during questioning, their friend Dill began to cry outside the courtroom, unable to bear it any longer.",
"Outside, a man named Dolphus Raymond, who liked to pretend he was drunk to explain his own choices to the disapproving town, kindly offered Dill a drink and explained his secret.",
"Back inside, Atticus stood before the jury and gave a powerful, calm speech, asking the twelve men to do their duty and judge the evidence honestly, without prejudice.",
"After many hours, the jury returned with their decision: guilty. Scout felt the whole room grow strangely silent and cold at the word.",
"As Atticus walked slowly out of the courtroom afterward, exhausted, every black person in the balcony rose respectfully to their feet in silent respect for him."
],
8:[
"After the guilty verdict, Jem felt completely crushed, unable to understand how twelve ordinary men on the jury could decide against Tom Robinson despite all the careful evidence Atticus had shown them in court.",
"Quietly, over the following days, many black families in Maycomb sent food to the Finch house: eggs, vegetables, and even a whole chicken, as their own gentle way of thanking Atticus for his hard work on Tom's case.",
"Soon afterward, an angry Bob Ewell stopped Atticus outside the post office, decided to spit directly in his face on the street, and swore he would get his revenge, no matter how long it took him.",
"Despite everything, Atticus stayed calm, holding onto hope, and explained to the children that he planned to appeal the court's decision to a higher court and try for a fairer, more honest result next time.",
"That evening, Jem and Atticus, both thoughtful, sat and talked for a long time about how juries worked and why good men sometimes made unfair decisions, while Scout quietly began thinking about the different kinds of people in the world.",
"At Aunt Alexandra's missionary tea, surrounded by respectable ladies discussing faraway problems, shocking news suddenly arrived at the front door: Tom Robinson had been shot several times while trying to escape from the prison yard.",
"The whole gathering fell silent and sad at once, and Atticus, when he came to tell Calpurnia and Aunt Alexandra himself, looked more tired and defeated than anyone in the family had ever seen him before.",
"In the local newspaper the next day, kind Mr. Underwood wrote a powerful editorial comparing Tom Robinson's senseless death to the pointless killing of harmless songbirds that never did anything but sing."
],
9:[
"Back at school, Scout's teacher Miss Gates spoke angrily against Hitler's cruel treatment of Jewish people in Germany, which confused Scout, since she had heard the same teacher say unkind things about black neighbors after the trial. Scout began to notice how unfair this seemed, though she did not fully understand it yet, and she asked Jem confusing questions about it.",
"Meanwhile, Bob Ewell lost his job and blamed Atticus and the court for his troubles. He tried to break into Judge Taylor's house one night and continued to harass Tom Robinson's widow, Helen.",
"At the school Halloween pageant, Scout dressed in an awkward ham costume, and everyone laughed when she stumbled onto the stage at the wrong moment during the show.",
"Walking home together in the dark after the pageant, still wearing her heavy costume, Jem and Scout suddenly heard strange footsteps following them closely.",
"Someone decided to attack the children fiercely in the dark, and during the struggle Jem's arm was broken badly, leaving Scout terrified and unable to see clearly inside her costume.",
"A silent stranger appeared out of nowhere, fought off the attacker bravely, and then began to carry the injured Jem all the way home to safety.",
"Later that night, Sheriff Heck Tate arrived and found Bob Ewell lying dead in the dirt beneath the tree, with a kitchen knife stuck under his ribs."
],
10:[
"At home, Scout finally saw the pale, quiet man standing in the corner of Jem's bedroom, and she suddenly began to realize that this gentle stranger was Boo Radley himself.",
"Atticus believed that Jem had killed Bob Ewell to protect Scout, and he wanted to confess the truth honestly to the sheriff, no matter the consequences.",
"But Sheriff Heck Tate firmly insisted that Bob Ewell had simply fallen on his own knife during the struggle, and that this was the only story anyone needed to know.",
"Heck Tate explained that to drag shy, private Boo Radley into a public trial as a hero would be deeply cruel, almost like killing a mockingbird that only ever wanted peace and quiet.",
"Atticus finally understood and quietly agreed to protect Boo's privacy, thanking Heck Tate for his wisdom and kindness.",
"Grateful for everything Boo had secretly done for her family over the years, Scout gently took his pale hand and offered to walk him home.",
"Together, they walked slowly across the yard toward the Radley house, and Scout felt oddly proud to be walking beside her mysterious neighbor at last.",
"Standing quietly on the Radley porch afterward, Scout looked back at her own street from Boo's point of view, and for the first time she truly understood what her father had once told her about imagining someone else's life."
]
};
